-- title:
--   Fontaine's unique lifting of logarithm-type coordinates
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $\mathcal O$ a commutative ring, $d$ a natural number, and for each $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,d$ and $n \in \mathbb N$ let $a_{i,n} \in \mathcal O[[X_1,\dots,X_d]]$ be a power series with vanishing constant coefficient. Write $L_n$ for the $d \times d$ matrix over $\mathcal O$ whose $(i,j)$ entry is the coefficient of $X_j$ in $a_{i,n}$ (that is, [`MvFormalGroup.linearPart`](def/MvFormalGroup_BasicV2.html#L359) applied to $i \mapsto a_{i,n}$). Assume the reduction of $L_0$ modulo the ideal $(p) = \mathrm{span}\{p\}$ of $\mathcal O$ is invertible over $\mathcal O/(p)$, and assume in addition that either $p \neq 2$, or else this reduction of $L_0$ is the identity matrix and every entry $(L_1)_{ij}$ with $j \le i$ lies in $(p) \subseteq \mathcal O$. Let $g$ be a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra in which $p$ is a non-zero-divisor and which is adically complete (and separated) for the ideal $pg$. Let $x^0 : \mathrm{Fin}\,d \to g$ have all components in the radical of $pg$, and let $\xi : \mathrm{Fin}\,d \to g[1/p]$ (the localisation of $g$ away from $p$) satisfy, for each $i$, that $\xi_i - W_i(x^0)$ lies in [`Deformation.PLoc.pSub p g`](def/Dieudonne_FontaineFunctor.html#L58), the $g$-submodule of $g[1/p]$ generated by the image of $p$; here $W_i(x)$ denotes [`Deformation.PLoc.wSeries`](def/Dieudonne_FontaineFunctor.html#L233) applied to $n \mapsto$ [`MvFormalGroup.adicEval`](def/MvFormalGroup_PointsV2.html#L20) of $a_{i,n}$ at $x$ for the ideal $pg$, i.e. the $p$-adic limit in $g[1/p]$ of the partial sums $\sum_{n<N} p^{-n} a_{i,n}(x)^{p^n}$ when such a limit exists and $0$ otherwise. The conclusion is that there is exactly one $x : \mathrm{Fin}\,d \to g$ with $x_j - x^0_j \in pg$ for all $j$ and $W_i(x) = \xi_i$ for all $i$.
--
--   This is the analytic core of Fontaine's lifting lemma for logarithm-type series $\sum_n p^{-n} a_n(X)^{p^n}$ evaluated at topologically nilpotent points of a $p$-adically complete ring, in the form needed for the connected coordinates of a special ring, including the case $p = 2$ under the normal-form hypothesis on $L_0$ and $L_1$. It is used in the Honda-system part of the deformation theory, by [`Deformation.HondaSystem.existsUnique_coords_of_mem_fontaineFunctor_of_splitCoordinates`](thm.html#Deformation.HondaSystem.existsUnique_coords_of_mem_fontaineFunctor_of_splitCoordinates).
